1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,802
An engineer has spent an
afternoon on a failed experiment.

2
00:00:07,842 --> 00:00:12,238
Twenty files are changed, the tests are
red, and in frustration they type git

3
00:00:12,278 --> 00:00:14,485
restore . at the top of the repository.

4
00:00:14,825 --> 00:00:19,941
Nineteen of those changes were worthless.
One was the fix for a bug they had found

5
00:00:19,981 --> 00:00:25,034
on the way. It's gone. No commit, meaning
no saved snapshot of the project, holds

6
00:00:25,074 --> 00:00:30,002
it. No reflog, Git's local journal of
where your branches pointed, recorded the

7
00:00:30,042 --> 00:00:32,140
event. And Git has no undo for it.

8
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:39,269
git restore . at the top level of a repository
is the working-tree half of git reset

9
00:00:39,309 --> 00:00:43,753
--hard. The usual trigger is frustration,
and the usual loss is the one useful

10
00:00:43,793 --> 00:00:48,410
change among twenty useless ones. Today
you learn exactly where git restore takes

11
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:53,182
its content from, what it overwrites, and
the one case in which something survives.

12
00:00:55,022 --> 00:00:58,675
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:58,715 --> 00:01:03,761
Today: three commands that change the working
tree, and one fact about what Git does

14
00:01:03,801 --> 00:01:04,367
not store.

15
00:01:04,707 --> 00:01:07,935
git restore overwrites files
with a stored version.

16
00:01:07,975 --> 00:01:12,870
git mv and git rm move and remove files,
and tell the index in the same step.

17
00:01:12,910 --> 00:01:18,125
And then a question that sounds like trivia
and isn't: does Git record that a file

18
00:01:18,165 --> 00:01:23,508
was renamed? It doesn't. You'll prove that
by opening a commit, and you'll see where

19
00:01:23,548 --> 00:01:26,392
the word "rename" in
Git's output comes from.

20
00:01:26,732 --> 00:01:31,696
First, a five-second reminder of three
places. The working tree is the files on your

21
00:01:31,736 --> 00:01:36,580
disk. The index, or staging area, is Git's
list of what goes into the next commit,

22
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:41,285
and git add copies a file into it: that's
staging. And HEAD is the last commit.

23
00:01:43,125 --> 00:01:44,703
Here's what you'll walk away with.

24
00:01:45,043 --> 00:01:46,456
After this video you can:

25
00:01:46,796 --> 00:01:51,353
Say where git restore <path> takes its
content from, and what it overwrites without

26
00:01:51,393 --> 00:01:51,962
a way back.

27
00:01:52,302 --> 00:01:55,141
Restore a path from HEAD
or from an older commit.

28
00:01:55,481 --> 00:01:58,665
Show that git mv equals a
manual move plus staging.

29
00:01:59,005 --> 00:02:03,664
Prove that a commit contains no rename
record, and explain the similarity score.

30
00:02:05,504 --> 00:02:10,537
git restore, in one sentence. git restore
<path> overwrites files in the working

31
00:02:10,577 --> 00:02:15,675
tree with a stored version: the version
in the index by default, the version in a

32
00:02:15,715 --> 00:02:20,685
commit with --source. Watch version three:
edited, never staged, and wiped out.

33
00:02:21,025 --> 00:02:24,689
It's 🔴 DANGEROUS, so the five
questions come before anything else.

34
00:02:25,029 --> 00:02:30,142
What it changes: files in the working
tree, and index entries with --staged.

35
00:02:30,482 --> 00:02:35,355
What it can destroy: every unstaged change
in the named paths, without confirmation.

36
00:02:35,695 --> 00:02:41,360
How to preview: git diff -- <path> shows
exactly what a restore from the index will

37
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:46,033
discard. git diff <commit> -- <path>
shows it for --source=<commit>.

38
00:02:46,373 --> 00:02:51,377
How to recover: not through Git, unless the
content was staged or committed at some

39
00:02:51,417 --> 00:02:54,416
point. Editor history and
backups are outside Git.

40
00:02:54,756 --> 00:02:59,412
When it is appropriate: after you've read
the diff and decided that the changes are

41
00:02:59,452 --> 00:03:04,051
worthless. When in doubt, commit to a
throwaway branch, or stash, which parks your

42
00:03:04,091 --> 00:03:06,314
uncommitted work. Both keep the content.

43
00:03:06,654 --> 00:03:11,819
Two choices define a restore. First,
where the content comes from.

44
00:03:11,859 --> 00:03:16,945
Without --source, that's the index,
or HEAD if --staged is given.

45
00:03:16,985 --> 00:03:22,860
With --source=<tree>, it's that commit
or tree. Second, where it's written.

46
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:27,040
The working tree by default.
The index with --staged.

47
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:31,299
Both, when --staged and
--worktree are given together.

48
00:03:31,639 --> 00:03:36,087
Quick quiz, two options. You staged
a change this morning and forgot it.

49
00:03:36,127 --> 00:03:41,199
Now you edit the file again, dislike it,
and run git restore on it, with no flags.

50
00:03:41,239 --> 00:03:45,188
Do you get the last commit, or
what you staged? Say your answer.

51
00:03:50,028 --> 00:03:54,618
What you staged. Without --source,
the content comes from the index.

52
00:03:54,658 --> 00:03:59,452
Almost everyone says the last commit
the first time, and that's normal.

53
00:03:59,792 --> 00:04:05,645
Two details. The command needs at least one
pathspec, meaning a path or pattern that

54
00:04:05,685 --> 00:04:11,258
selects files. And the default mode is
"no overlay": a tracked path that doesn't

55
00:04:11,298 --> 00:04:17,081
exist in the source is removed, so that the
destination matches the source exactly.

56
00:04:17,121 --> 00:04:22,834
That has a sharp edge. git restore
--source=HEAD~5 src/ removes every tracked file

57
00:04:22,874 --> 00:04:28,727
under src/ that didn't exist five commits
ago, together with any unstaged changes in

58
00:04:28,767 --> 00:04:34,269
them. So name files, not directories,
when you take content from an old commit.

59
00:04:34,609 --> 00:04:40,233
Inside .git. Restoring the working tree from
the index reads the blob that the index

60
00:04:40,273 --> 00:04:45,492
entry names, and writes it to disk. A
blob is the object that holds one file's

61
00:04:45,532 --> 00:04:50,414
content. No object is created, and no
ref, meaning no branch name, moves.

62
00:04:50,454 --> 00:04:55,538
So no reflog records the event. The
content that was overwritten isn't saved

63
00:04:55,578 --> 00:04:56,144
anywhere.

64
00:04:56,484 --> 00:05:01,633
What survives. One exception is worth
knowing. Anything that was ever staged went

65
00:05:01,673 --> 00:05:06,885
into the object database, Git's store inside
the dot git folder, when git add ran.

66
00:05:06,925 --> 00:05:12,009
git fsck can find it as a dangling blob.
Dangling objects are removed by garbage

67
00:05:12,049 --> 00:05:17,133
collection, Git's clean-up of unused
objects, after a grace period, two weeks by

68
00:05:17,173 --> 00:05:20,656
default. So this is a rescue
route, not a storage plan.

69
00:05:20,996 --> 00:05:26,355
git mv and git rm. In one sentence: they
do in one step what you can do in two.

70
00:05:26,395 --> 00:05:30,933
You change the working tree with
mv or rm, then you tell the index.

71
00:05:31,273 --> 00:05:36,579
git mv <source> <destination> is 🟢 SAFE: it
renames the file on disk and renames its

72
00:05:36,619 --> 00:05:41,924
index entry. git rm <path> is 🟡 CAUTION: it
deletes the file on disk and removes its

73
00:05:41,964 --> 00:05:47,015
index entry, and it refuses when the file's
content isn't safely stored in HEAD.

74
00:05:47,055 --> 00:05:51,343
With -f it overrides that check,
and is 🔴 DANGEROUS for that reason.

75
00:05:51,383 --> 00:05:55,925
--cached removes only the index entry.
Neither command creates a commit.

76
00:05:55,965 --> 00:05:59,807
The index is updated, and the
change must still be committed.

77
00:06:00,147 --> 00:06:04,477
Two behaviors that the textbook's
authors found while writing the demos.

78
00:06:04,517 --> 00:06:08,301
git rm deletes a directory when
it removes the last file in it.

79
00:06:08,341 --> 00:06:11,821
And git mv doesn't create a
missing destination directory.

80
00:06:12,161 --> 00:06:16,908
No renames. In one sentence: a commit
stores a snapshot of paths and contents, so

81
00:06:16,948 --> 00:06:21,696
there's no place in it where "this file
used to be called that" could be written.

82
00:06:21,736 --> 00:06:26,069
A rename is a conclusion that Git draws
later, by comparing two snapshots.

83
00:06:26,409 --> 00:06:31,662
The Git User's Manual says it directly: Git
"does not attempt to record file renames

84
00:06:31,702 --> 00:06:36,829
explicitly, though it can identify cases
where the existence of the same file data

85
00:06:36,869 --> 00:06:39,160
at changing paths suggests a rename".

86
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:44,891
When a diff, a comparison of two snapshots,
is computed, a deleted path and an added

87
00:06:44,931 --> 00:06:49,804
path with similar content are reported
as a rename, with a similarity score.

88
00:06:49,844 --> 00:06:54,200
R100 means 100 percent similar. The
default threshold is 50 percent.

89
00:06:54,540 --> 00:06:59,325
Why this matters. Three habits follow
from detection being a heuristic, a good

90
00:06:59,365 --> 00:07:04,212
guess. First, rename in one commit and
edit in the next when the edit is large.

91
00:07:04,252 --> 00:07:09,099
A rename combined with a rewrite can fall
below the threshold, and then git log

92
00:07:09,139 --> 00:07:14,172
--follow, git blame and the rename handling
in merges all treat it as an unrelated

93
00:07:14,212 --> 00:07:14,728
new file.

94
00:07:15,068 --> 00:07:18,988
Second, expect very large moves
to look like delete plus add.

95
00:07:19,028 --> 00:07:24,050
The exhaustive part of detection is
skipped when the number of candidate files

96
00:07:24,090 --> 00:07:28,594
exceeds diff.renameLimit, whose
default the 2.55 manual gives as 1000.

97
00:07:28,634 --> 00:07:33,332
Third, don't look for a "rename" flag
to audit. There's none in the data.

98
00:07:35,172 --> 00:07:40,355
A picture helps. For git restore, the
textbook's analogy is "revert to saved" in an

99
00:07:40,395 --> 00:07:44,068
editor, where "saved" means
"last staged". It breaks twice.

100
00:07:44,108 --> 00:07:49,102
There's no undo afterwards. And "saved"
is the index, not the last commit, which

101
00:07:49,142 --> 00:07:52,689
surprises people who have
staged something and forgotten.

102
00:07:53,029 --> 00:07:56,963
For renames, think of two photographs
of a room, taken a day apart.

103
00:07:57,003 --> 00:08:01,945
In the first, a chair stands by the window.
In the second, an identical chair stands

104
00:08:01,985 --> 00:08:06,630
by the door, and there's none by the
window. You say "someone moved the chair".

105
00:08:06,670 --> 00:08:11,256
Nothing in either photograph says so.
It's your inference from comparing them.

106
00:08:11,296 --> 00:08:16,119
And if the chair had also been repainted,
you might have said "someone removed one

107
00:08:16,159 --> 00:08:19,974
chair and brought another". The
rename is the reader's inference.

108
00:08:21,814 --> 00:08:23,308
Now with real file names.

109
00:08:23,648 --> 00:08:29,006
On the left, the old tree lists
src/retriever.py. A tree is Git's record of one

110
00:08:29,046 --> 00:08:32,969
directory. On the right, the
new tree lists src/search.py.

111
00:08:33,009 --> 00:08:37,684
Both entries point at one blob.
Neither tree mentions the other name.

112
00:08:37,724 --> 00:08:43,150
When Git compares the two trees, it sees
a path that disappeared and a path that

113
00:08:43,190 --> 00:08:46,976
appeared with the same content,
and it reports a rename.

114
00:08:48,816 --> 00:08:52,610
One staged line, then one
unstaged line in the same file.

115
00:08:52,650 --> 00:08:58,259
Status will say MM, one letter for each
change. The five questions for git restore 🔴

116
00:08:58,299 --> 00:09:02,159
DANGEROUS have been answered,
and the preview is git diff.

117
00:09:02,199 --> 00:09:07,741
Predict: after git restore config/settings.yaml,
which of the two lines is still in

118
00:09:07,781 --> 00:09:10,230
the file? Say it out loud. I'll wait.

119
00:09:15,070 --> 00:09:19,280
The staged line won. The file
now equals the index, not HEAD.

120
00:09:19,320 --> 00:09:23,531
The staged max_tokens line
survived, and debug: true is gone.

121
00:09:23,871 --> 00:09:27,868
Now try it, on paper, thirty
seconds. Draw these three boxes.

122
00:09:27,908 --> 00:09:32,765
Next I run git restore --source=HEAD
on the same file, with no other flag.

123
00:09:32,805 --> 00:09:36,934
Which boxes change, and which two
status letters do you expect?

124
00:09:36,974 --> 00:09:39,051
Pause me, and write your answer.

125
00:09:43,891 --> 00:09:49,143
Only the working tree. With --source, the
content comes from a commit and, without

126
00:09:49,183 --> 00:09:54,563
--staged, only the working tree is written.
So after the first command the status is

127
00:09:54,603 --> 00:09:59,726
MM: the index still holds the staged line,
and the working tree now lacks it, so

128
00:09:59,766 --> 00:10:05,017
both comparisons differ. Then --staged
--worktree writes both places, and the path

129
00:10:05,057 --> 00:10:05,598
is clean.

130
00:10:05,938 --> 00:10:08,581
A deleted tracked file
comes back the same way.

131
00:10:08,921 --> 00:10:10,532
Any commit can be the source.

132
00:10:10,872 --> 00:10:15,118
The older spelling, shown once.
There's one difference that matters.

133
00:10:15,158 --> 00:10:20,348
git checkout <commit> -- <path> writes the
index as well as the working tree, which

134
00:10:20,388 --> 00:10:25,516
is the M in the first column here. git
restore --source=<commit> <path> wrote only

135
00:10:25,556 --> 00:10:26,588
the working tree.

136
00:10:26,928 --> 00:10:31,757
Now the rescue route. The line that was
staged in the first step and then discarded:

137
00:10:31,797 --> 00:10:32,452
where is it?

138
00:10:32,792 --> 00:10:38,398
git fsck reports a dangling blob, e4eb0e6:
an object that no ref, commit or index

139
00:10:38,438 --> 00:10:42,650
entry points to. It's the staged
version from the first step.

140
00:10:42,690 --> 00:10:48,366
That's the survivor I promised you. The
unstaged debug: true line was never added,

141
00:10:48,406 --> 00:10:49,760
so nothing holds it.

142
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:55,919
The index before and after a git mv 🟢
SAFE. git ls-files --stage lists the index

143
00:10:55,959 --> 00:10:56,504
entries.

144
00:10:56,844 --> 00:11:01,989
The entry kept its blob ID and changed its
path. git write-tree prints the ID of the

145
00:11:02,029 --> 00:11:07,050
tree that this index describes. Remember
that ID. The script now undoes everything

146
00:11:07,090 --> 00:11:11,865
and performs the rename with shell
commands. Predict the tree ID: the same, or

147
00:11:11,905 --> 00:11:13,531
different? Say it out loud.

148
00:11:18,371 --> 00:11:23,263
The same. The tree ID is identical,
beginning 566379b both times.

149
00:11:23,303 --> 00:11:28,878
That's the proof that git mv adds
nothing that mv plus git add -A doesn't.

150
00:11:29,218 --> 00:11:34,272
Now commit the rename and open the commit.
If Git recorded renames, the record would

151
00:11:34,312 --> 00:11:34,758
be here.

152
00:11:35,098 --> 00:11:40,411
The summary line says rename, with 100
percent. But the commit object has a tree, a

153
00:11:40,451 --> 00:11:44,474
parent, two identities and a
message, and the tree lists paths.

154
00:11:44,514 --> 00:11:46,215
No field mentions a rename.

155
00:11:46,555 --> 00:11:51,528
The word comes from the comparison. R100
means "a deleted path and an added path

156
00:11:51,568 --> 00:11:56,666
whose contents are 100 percent similar".
Turn detection off with --no-renames, and

157
00:11:56,706 --> 00:11:59,987
the same two commits show a
deletion and an addition.

158
00:12:00,327 --> 00:12:04,151
Rename and edit in one commit:
the similarity drops to 96.

159
00:12:04,191 --> 00:12:08,947
Raise the threshold to 98 percent,
and the rename is no longer reported.

160
00:12:08,987 --> 00:12:12,345
So whether you see a rename
depends on a threshold.

161
00:12:12,685 --> 00:12:16,491
History that follows a file
depends on the same detection.

162
00:12:16,531 --> 00:12:21,861
Without --follow, the log of src/vector_search.py
starts at the commit where that

163
00:12:21,901 --> 00:12:26,568
path first appears. With it, Git
detects the two renames and continues.

164
00:12:26,608 --> 00:12:29,087
--follow works for a single file only.

165
00:12:29,427 --> 00:12:33,307
And git rm 🟡 CAUTION, with the
safety check that plain rm lacks.

166
00:12:33,347 --> 00:12:38,207
The first removal went through, because
the file matched HEAD, so the content is

167
00:12:38,247 --> 00:12:43,352
recoverable. The second was refused, because
the file had a modification that exists

168
00:12:43,392 --> 00:12:44,148
nowhere else.

169
00:12:45,988 --> 00:12:47,636
Five mistakes to watch for.

170
00:12:47,976 --> 00:12:52,260
Edits disappear after git restore
<path>. Root cause: the working tree was

171
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:56,817
overwritten from the index, and content
that was never staged is in no object.

172
00:12:57,157 --> 00:13:01,256
Expecting git restore <path> to
return the last committed version.

173
00:13:01,296 --> 00:13:06,085
Root cause: without --source the content
comes from the index, which may hold

174
00:13:06,125 --> 00:13:08,092
something you staged and forgot.

175
00:13:08,432 --> 00:13:12,431
git restore --source=<old commit>
<directory> deletes files.

176
00:13:12,471 --> 00:13:18,018
Root cause: restore runs in no-overlay mode,
so tracked paths that the source lacks

177
00:13:18,058 --> 00:13:18,826
are removed.

178
00:13:19,166 --> 00:13:24,113
The history of a file stops at a rename.
Root cause: the rename commit also rewrote

179
00:13:24,153 --> 00:13:29,039
the file, and the similarity fell below
the threshold; rename and edit in separate

180
00:13:29,079 --> 00:13:29,520
commits.

181
00:13:29,860 --> 00:13:35,192
Using git rm --cached to unstage a change.
Root cause: it removes the index entry,

182
00:13:35,232 --> 00:13:40,302
and the next commit deletes the path for
everyone else; it is the tool to stop

183
00:13:40,342 --> 00:13:41,350
tracking a file.

184
00:13:43,190 --> 00:13:47,374
Now, out of the lab. A retrieval team moves
a module to a new package and, in the

185
00:13:47,414 --> 00:13:51,546
same commit, rewrites most of it. Months
later someone runs git blame on the new

186
00:13:51,586 --> 00:13:55,770
file to find out why a threshold has its
value, and every line points at the move

187
00:13:55,810 --> 00:13:56,135
commit.

188
00:13:56,475 --> 00:14:00,826
git log --follow stops there too.
Nothing is lost: the old commits exist.

189
00:14:00,866 --> 00:14:05,698
But the tools that connect the two names
depend on similarity, and the similarity

190
00:14:05,738 --> 00:14:10,510
fell below the threshold. The team's rule
after that is the textbook's: a commit

191
00:14:10,550 --> 00:14:12,855
that renames, then a commit that edits.

192
00:14:13,195 --> 00:14:18,354
And for restores, the habit from the
textbook: when the changes are mixed, use git

193
00:14:18,394 --> 00:14:22,983
restore -p to discard hunk by hunk,
one block of changed lines at a time.

194
00:14:23,023 --> 00:14:27,104
A stash or a throwaway commit costs
seconds and keeps everything.

195
00:14:28,944 --> 00:14:36,006
Your turn. Do Exercise 2.6, Level 2, "a
move and an edit, as two commits", in

196
00:14:36,046 --> 00:14:39,050
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

197
00:14:39,390 --> 00:14:43,878
Before each commit, predict what git diff
--name-status between the commit and its

198
00:14:43,918 --> 00:14:48,074
parent will print, including the similarity
score where you expect a rename.

199
00:14:49,914 --> 00:14:55,532
Q47: "Prove to me that Git does not store
renames. Then explain what R087 in a diff

200
00:14:55,572 --> 00:14:58,736
means and name two features
that depend on it."

201
00:15:01,076 --> 00:15:05,730
Answer out loud. The word "prove" is the
test. A strong answer names the commands

202
00:15:05,770 --> 00:15:10,076
that open a commit and its tree, and
says what you would find and not find.

203
00:15:10,116 --> 00:15:13,379
Then it shows the same pair
of commits reported two ways.

204
00:15:13,419 --> 00:15:18,131
For the second half, explain the letter
and the number separately, and connect the

205
00:15:18,171 --> 00:15:19,406
number to a threshold.

206
00:15:21,246 --> 00:15:26,129
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say, in your own words: git restore

207
00:15:26,169 --> 00:15:30,989
<path> overwrites the working-tree file
